            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does whey protein have sugar?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey contains lactose, milk's natural sugar. Whey concentrate usually has 2 to 3 g per serving, while whey isolate is filtered to remove most of it and often shows 0 g or under 1 g. Flavored wheys may also add sugar, so check both the sugar and added sugars lines and the ingredient list.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are sugar alcohols in protein powder bad?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for most people in small amounts, but they can cause gas, bloating or loose stools, especially xylitol and maltitol. Erythritol has little effect on blood sugar, though a 2023 study linked high blood levels with heart events, an association not shown to be causal. If you are sensitive, choose a powder without them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is sugar-free protein powder good for diabetics?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can be a useful option, but sugar is only one factor. Check total carbohydrate, watch for maltodextrin and consider what you mix it with. Protein can also affect insulin. People with diabetes, especially those on insulin or similar medication, should check with their doctor or dietitian and test their own response.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is sugar-free protein powder keto?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most sugar-free whey isolates fit a keto diet easily, with 0 to 3 g of total carbohydrate per scoop. Powders sweetened with erythritol usually fit too, since erythritol is generally excluded from net carbs. Avoid products with maltodextrin or large amounts of maltitol, and check total carbs, not just sugar.
                </p>
              </div>
